Well, America's a big country.  While big media gives us a 
very bland face on TV going for the lowest-common-denominator, 
in isolated pockets and communities you can still hear almost 
every linguistic construct that's ever been common in English.

"Score" is still in live parlance in a few isolated areas; known 
to almost everyone; recognized as a part of formal and/or old-
fashioned speech.  It's famous from often-quoted speeches 
("Fourscore and seven years ago, our forefathers brought forth...")  
from the classical Age Of Man ("Threescore years and ten") and 
from a whole bunch of games that are over the minute someone's 
score gets higher than a "score" of points.  Things used to 
be sold by the score in 5x4 cases, but these days packaging 
decisions are made on a very individual basis and the number of 
items in a case is always stamped on the case - even if the 
number happens to be "20."

My grandfather used "score" meaning twenty about as often as he
used "dozen" for twelve. It was a marked use even in his time, 
but everybody understood him. He also used "Great Gross" to 
mean a dozen times a gross, but people outside his isolated 
community usually came up blank on that one. 

